N2  - Introduction: A 32-year-old female presented to the pain center for refractory atypical facial pain distributed in the right mandibular nerve region. She had a history of trigeminal neuralgia with multiple therapies including microvascular decompression x3; gamma knife treatments; balloon compression; trigeminal tractotomy; and deep brain stimulation targeting the VC, PBG, and subgenual cingulate. She presented specifically for the neuropathic pain in the mandibular nerve distribution. After discussion of risks, we agreed to the placement of an off label percutaneous nerve stimulator to the mandibular nerve. Methods: The literature and medical record were reviewed. As the case report is devoid of patient identifiable information and consists of less than five patients, it is exempt from IRB review requirements as per Emory policy. Results: Using a modified Kantorowicz technique, a percutaneous nerve stimulator was placed from a submandibular approach under general anesthesia. The patient reported 50% relief of her pain but struggled with the adhesive and dressing placement. Unfortunately lead migration occurred after 4 weeks and the PNS array was replaced using a similar approach, but tunneled to allow a dressing further down her neck. The patient was able to tolerate therapy, and stimulation maintained 50% relief of her pain for the 60-day placement with minimal durable relief. Conclusion: Trigeminal nerve stimulation remains off-label and there are limited reports of the usage of percutaneous nerve stimulators. This case reports describes the usage of the Kantorowicz technique to position an electrode array in a manner to capture the mandibular nerve as well as a simple tunneling technique to better anchor the lead with the increased mobility of the face/neck region. Disclosures: Tawanna Charlton, MD: None, Yawar Qadri, MD, PhD: None
