N2  - Functional SRS-Pain (Radiosurgery Parallel Session), October 14, 2024, 8:00 AM - 9:30 AM Background: In the present work we relate recent advances in experimental studies of an impact of microvascular decompression (MVD) and balloon compression (BC) on electrical activity (EA) in the trigeminal nerve (TN) root and Gasserian ganglion (GG) in patients with trigeminal neuralgia (TGN). Methods: 15 TGN patients participated in the work. Using irritation on the face, EA in the TN root and GG was investigated before and after MVD and BC. 4 patients with posterior cranial fossa tumors without TGN signs were included in a control group. Results: In all patients spontaneous EA (without any irritation on the face) in the TN root was not detected, whereas evoked EA in the TN root and GG was registered before MVD and BC in response to superficial or deep irritation on the face. After the surgical procedures (6 patients after MVD and 1 patient after BC) the EA was significantly decreased along with the facial pain. In the remaining patients after MVD (3 patients), evoked EA in the TN root was still identified simultaneously with the pain paroxysms after surgery. In the control group, spontaneous or evoked EA was not determined. Conclusions: Evoked EA in the TN was detected only in TGN patients in response to irritation on the face. After the surgical procedures postoperative pain relief was associated with EA decreasing in the TN root and GG.
