N2  - Objectives: To explore the clinical efficacy and risks of trigeminal nerve sensory root combing (IN) IN the treatment of primary trigeminal neuralgia (PTN), summarize the management skills of different subtypes of PTN based on neurovascular compression (NVC), and the strategies to deal with the risks during IN manipulation, in order to provide reference for the individualized surgical treatment of PTN patients. Methods: The clinical data of 108 PTN patients who received IN from July 2015 to November 2019 IN neurosurgery Department of Anhui Provincial Hospital were retrospectively analyzed. Patients were grouped according to the type of NVC found intraoperatively. During the operation, the cistern segment of the trigeminal nerve sensory root was dissected into multiple nerve bundles (usually 5 ∼ 7) paralleling the direction of the nerve root. According to the type of NVC, the corresponding operation is performed. Results: Among 108 PTN patients, 24 patients with nonresponsible vascular type, 17 patients with recurrent type, 7 patients with simple venous type (7 cases /19 cases), and 2 patients with combined arteriovenous type (2 cases /21 cases) received simple IN. Microvascular decompression (MVD) +IN was performed IN 12 patients with simple venous type (12 cases /19 cases), 19 patients with combined arteriovenous type (19 cases /21 cases), and 27 patients with uncertain responsible vessels. During the operation, attention should be paid to the protection of the superior petrosal vein complex (SPVC), fully expose the trigeminal nerve and release its surrounding arachnoid adhesion to avoid the omission of responsible vessels. The overall effective rate was 94.4% (102/108) after 3-24 months follow-up, and some patients had delayed cure. There were no significant complication after operation, 4 cases had recurrence and 2 cases had incomplete relief of pain, which were cured by PBC. Postoperative facial hypesthesia was 58.3% (63/108). Ninety-four patients (87.0%) developed introoperative TCR, and no serious hemodynamic abnormalities were found through cooperation with anesthesiologists. Conclusions: IN is suitable for the treatment of PTN of different NVC types, with good and stable clinical efficacy, controllable risk and slight postoperative complications, and can be used as an effective supplement to MVD.
