N2  - One sentence summary: This study presents the first case describing successful treatment of refractory atypical trigeminal neuralgia using rimegepant, an orally administered small molecule calcitonin gene-related peptide receptor antagonist. Background: Trigeminal neuralgia (TN), the most common facial pain syndrome, is characterized by episodes of severe stabbing or shock-like pain, lasting seconds to minutes, along the distribution of the trigeminal nerve. Atypical TN presents as constant, burning pain along the same nerve distribution and is more treatment-resistant than classical TN. Despite the availability of multiple medical (anticonvulsants, muscle relaxants, opioids) and surgical (rhizotomy or microvascular decompression) interventions, the efficacy of long-term treatment for patients with TN is limited. Evidence suggests that calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) may be involved in the pathophysiology of classical TN, and positive results in patients with TN have been reported with erenumab, a CGRP monoclonal antibody. We present the first case describing the successful treatment of a patient with atypical TN using rimegepant, an orally administered small molecule CGRP receptor antagonist. Methods: Data regarding disease history, treatment course, and outcomes were collected through manual chart review and a patient interview. Results: A 56-year- old male with a 5-year history of TN presented with episodic sharp, stabbing, and constant burning pain along the V2 nerve distribution. Self-reported pain score ranged from 7 to 9 out of 10; Barrow Neurological Institute (BNI) pain intensity score was IV out of V. Treatment history included failure of multiple pain medications and surgical procedures, including Gamma Knife radiosurgery, right balloon rhizotomy, radiofrequency ablation, and V2-3 radiofrequency rhizotomy. Medical treatment at presentation was 300 mg oxcarbazepine daily, up to 120 mg oxycodone (≤8 × 15 mg tablets) daily, and 10 mcg per hour transdermal buprenorphine patch. Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrated enhancement of V2, suggesting injury secondary to rhizotomy (Figure). Microvascular decompression resolved the sharp, stabbing pain and reduced attack frequency and severity, but atypical TN pain persisted. After multiple trials of different medications for 6 years after the MVD, the patient was started on rimegepant 75 mg every other day. Within 12 hours of receiving the first dose of the CGRP receptor antagonist, self-reported pain score was 0 out of 10. Over the next 6 months, the patient experienced a near-absence of pain, and no significant adverse events were reported. Mild, occasional pain (2/10) was controlled with 1 mg buprenorphine. His BNI score improved to II out of V. The patient experienced dramatic improvements in mood and cognition and was weaned from several pain medications. Conclusion: Patients with TN experience substantial morbidity, and current treatments provide limited benefit for its atypical variant. The efficacy, safety, and tolerability of rimegepant described in this case suggest that orally administered small molecule calcitonin gene-related peptide receptor antagonists may provide benefits for patients with refractory atypical TN. (Figure Presented).
