N2  - Introduction: Chronic cephalgia and migraine headaches have been shown in the literature to respond favorably to high cervical spinal cord stimulation (SCS).1 Trigeminal neuralgia (TN) is the most prevalent form of facial pain and those with refractory disease are left with few treatment options. Surgical decompression, peripheral nerve stimulation (PNS) and nerve injections have been performed with limited success. Currently, high cervical spinal cord stimulation (SCS) is an emerging therapeutic technique that demonstrates sustained pain relief from chronic refractory TN. BurstDR stimulation is a novel pulse train that is programmed at paresthesia free amplitudes, decreasing some of the traditional drawbacks of high cervical stimulation. Methods: A retrospective analysis of a single patient suffering from chronic cervicalgia, occipital cephalgia and refractory TN was performed in conjunction with review of published case reports of cervical SCS for treatment of intractable headaches and chronic facial pain syndromes. Results: A 56 year old woman with a history of chronic right-sided occipital headaches, right-sided neck pain and TN in the right V2 and V3 distribution presented after failed balloon compression rhizotomy, sphenopalatine ganglion block, and microvascular decompression. There was no traumatic or inciting event that resulted in her pain. MRI of the cervical spine, temporomandibular joint (TMJ), and trigeminal nerve, did not show any structural lesions that would contribute to her pain syndrome. She had trialed multiple medications including opioids, NSAIDs, anticonvulsants, gabapentinoids and antidepressants all without great success. She received limited relief from occipital nerve blocks. After more than 5 years of chronic cervicalgia, occipital cephalgia, CRPS type II, and refractory TN, she underwent high cervical SCS trial using BurstDR stimulation at paresthesia free amplitudes and eventual permanent placement of SCS leads at the superior aspect of C2-3. One month post-operatively she experienced 70% pain relief and reported more facial coverage with the permanent placement compared to the SCS trial. Two weeks post-operatively she experienced mild facial numbness of the right V1 distribution that resolved one week later. At her one month follow up she reported enhanced daily function with an overall improvement in mental health. Conclusion: We present a patient with chronic cervicalgia, occipital cephalgia, CRPS and refractory TN who previously failed multiple treatment modalities and underwent a highly successful cervical SCS implantation which provided improved pain control and functional recovery. High cervical SCS using paresthesia free BurstDR stimulation is a treatment option to be considered in refractory TN and chronic cephalgia.
