N2  - Background and aims: Refractory trigeminal neuralgia (TN) refers to cases where no response to either carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine is apparent. There are currently different medical and surgical approaches to treat refractory TN. Our aim is to characterise medical and surgical therapies of refractory TN, focusing on response rate. Methods: We performed a retrospective descriptive analysis of TN cases followed by neurology pain specialists over a period of 6 years. Results: Our sample included 56 patients (41% idiopathic TN, 34% classic TN and 25% secondary TN). 66% of patients had purely paroxysmal TN. Only 14% of patients achieved pain control with one drug (carbamazepine in 63%); 20% needed 2 drugs and the remainder (66%) used at least 3 drugs. The initial drug of choice was carbamazepine in 63% of patients, followed by oxcarbazepine (16%) and pregabalin (11%). Gabapentin was second-line in 33%, followed by amitriptyline (29%) and pregabalin (23%). 45% of patients underwent surgery for TN (48% balloon compression, 28% microvascular decompression, 12% radiofrequency thermal lesion and 12% brain stereotactic radiosurgery). After surgery, 60% were pain-free, 24% had more tolerable pain and 16% did not improve. However 67% of patients experienced pain relapse afterwards. Surgery was repeated in 40% patients (80% of which once and 20% twice or more). Conclusion: A high percentage of TN patients were refractory to different drug combination, as in other series. Despite reducing and even eliminating pain for some time, surgical relapse rate was still significant, hinting that newer therapeutic options are needed.
