N2  - Surgical treatment (with either microvascular decompression or balloon decompression) may be offered to patients with trigeminal neuralgia resistant to medical therapy. Cranial nerve manipulation during these procedures may result in bradycardia [1]. Methods In a retrospective review of 47 patients who underwent a microvascular decompression or balloon compression between October 2013 and April 2016 in our institution we analysed the incidence of bradycardia. Bradycardia was defined as a heart rate < 60 (or a drop of > 20% if resting bradycardia before surgical stimulus). Results Forty-seven patients were identified (16 male, 29 female), the age range was 30-81 y. Thirty-two patients underwent microvascular decompression, 15 underwent balloon compression procedures and 27 of these 47 patients received glycopyrronium pre-treatment prior to surgical manipulation of the cranial nerve. None of the patients with vagolytic pre-treatment experienced bradycardic episodes; while 12 out of the 20 patients without prophylaxis experienced cardiac decelerations. Of those 12 patients, two had a brief asystole and a further two patients experienced a heart rate of 30/min or less. One patient was treated with atropine, and four received glycopyrronium to prevent further bradycardic episodes, the other patients settled with temporary cessation of the surgical stimulus. All patients recovered well. Discussion Without vagolytic pre-treatment, 12 patients (60%) undergoing surgical treatment for trigeminal neuralgia developed a short episode of bradycardia. Four patients (20%) without pre-treatment experienced a heart rate of 30/min or less at the time of surgical manipulation of the cranial nerve. None of the patients with vagolytic pre-treatment experienced a bradycardia. Given the frequency and possible severity of the bradycardia we recommend consideration of vagolytic prophylaxis for all patients undergoing these procedures.( Figure persented ).
