N2  - Introduction: Increasing costs of healthcare call for rational approaches based on cost effectiveness of the surgical procedures. When treating trigeminal neuralgia, therapeutical options vary widely as does their cost. Method: We have compared Microvascular decompression (MVD), Radiofrequency rhyzotomy (RFR), Percutaneous balloon compression of the Gasser ganglion (PBC) and Gamma knife rhizotomy (GKR) for length of stay, cost of the stay, of the procedure, of disposable material and of specific hospital investments. This was compared to the immediate and long term (>5 years) efficacy of the procedures. Results: The evaluated total cost were $1,014 for PBC and RFR, $3,360 for MVD with a 2 days hospital stay, $4,560 for MVD with a 5 days hospital stay, and $3,424 for GKR. In addition, RFR requires investing in a generator (10,000 €) and GKR requires a gamma knife suite (3,000,000 €). MVD, PBC and RFR allow immediate refief of the pain, GKS having a more progressive effect. Long term results however are comparable, all techniques having at least a 25 % recurrence rate between 5 and 10 year postoperatively. Conclusion: Although all surgical techniques allow pain relief in trigeminal neuralgia, from an economical point of view, percutaneous techniques are more cost effective than MVD and GKR. They should be considered as a first therapeutical option, keeping the more expensive procedures for percutaneous treatment failures or for medically justified indications.
