N2  - Trigeminal neuralgia, vago-glossopharyngeal neuralgia and facial spasm are the most relevant syndromes of vascular compression of cranial nerves. Nowadays the theory of vascular compression of the root entry zone of cranial nerves and the pathogenetic concept of ephaptic contacts between a-beta-fibers und c-fibers are the basis of modern conservative and operative therapeutic concepts. Causal surgery in form of microvascular decompression is not only the operative treatment of choice, but because of the excellent results also a fundamental support of the theory of vascular compression. For patients with trigeminal neuralgia, who can not tolerate major operations due to age or other reasons, percutaneous methods like thermocoagulation, glycerol rhizolysis and balloon compression of the Gasserian ganglion, stereotactic radiosurgery of the entry zone as well as electrical stimulation techniques are available. In the treatment of facial spasm especially in older patients botulinum toxin injection is an effective alternative procedure to vascular decompression of the facial nerve.
